1. accuracy - how accurate (clue's in the name) we are. not the best measurement, but a good good general overview.
why not the best? because a model may be 95% correct of the time towards benign samples, but all wrong towards malicious 

2. precision - how many were truly malicious out of all malicious samples we caught
precision mostly talks about whether we correctly identified truly malicious samples as malicious

3. recall - how many were caught based on all true attacks (this is crucial as FN here is missed attacks)
recall is useful since knowing FN here means these are the missed attacks the model is doing

4. f1 score - it checks whether both precision and recall is high (if either one is low, then f1 drops)

5. false positive rate - it checks what are classified as malicious when it actuality it is not

6. false negative rate - classified as benign but it is malicious

7. matthews correlation coefficient - stays informative. is not affected by class imbalances. solves the issue with accuracy being falsely good































Absolutely. In your trainer, all metrics come from the confusion matrix, computed in train_model.py and printed in train_model.py.

For your labels:
- Class 0 = benign
- Class 1 = malicious

First, confusion matrix terms:
- TN: benign predicted benign
- FP: benign predicted malicious (false alarm)
- FN: malicious predicted benign (missed attack)
- TP: malicious predicted malicious

1. Accuracy  
$$
\text{Accuracy}=\frac{TP+TN}{TP+TN+FP+FN}
$$
Relevance:
- Overall correctness.
- Good quick summary.
- Weakness: can look great even when one class is poorly handled (especially with imbalance).

2. Precision  
$$
\text{Precision}=\frac{TP}{TP+FP}
$$
Relevance:
- Of all alerts marked malicious, how many are truly malicious.
- High precision means fewer false alarms and better analyst trust.

3. Recall (Detection Rate)  
$$
\text{Recall}=\frac{TP}{TP+FN}
$$
Relevance:
- Of all true attacks, how many you actually catch.
- In security, this is critical because FN means missed attacks.

4. F1 Score  
$$
F1=2\cdot\frac{\text{Precision}\cdot\text{Recall}}{\text{Precision}+\text{Recall}}
$$
Relevance:
- Balances precision and recall into one number.
- Useful when you want good detection while keeping false alarms reasonable.

5. False Positive Rate (FPR)  
$$
\text{FPR}=\frac{FP}{FP+TN}
$$
Relevance:
- Among truly benign activity, how often you raise a malicious alert.
- Directly tied to alert fatigue in real deployment.

6. MCC (Matthews Correlation Coefficient)  
$$
\text{MCC}=
\frac{TP\cdot TN-FP\cdot FN}
{\sqrt{(TP+FP)(TP+FN)(TN+FP)(TN+FN)}}
$$
Relevance:
- A balanced single score in [-1, 1].
- 1 means perfect, 0 means random-like, -1 means total disagreement.
- Very useful when classes are imbalanced, often more trustworthy than accuracy alone.

How to interpret them together for NoEsc:
1. Prioritize Recall and FPR first.
2. Then check Precision.
3. Use F1 as balance.
4. Use MCC as overall quality sanity check.

